+# FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+Install it with pipx (Python 3.11 or newer): `pipx install tally-toolkit`. Check it with `tallyctl version`. If the
+command is not found, run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was called `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release; the old name works as an alias for one more release,
+then it is removed.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+Port 8731. The old port 8080 is closed, so anything still using it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+Run `tallyctl cache clear`. It clears all regions; add `--region NAME` to clear only one. The old purge command was
+removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement. The page goes from the primary on-call to the secondary, then to the engineering
+manager.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1, that is customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 incidents
+do not page; the primary picks them up in the morning. Keep an eye on the dashboards anyway.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Restart ingest with a drain first and look for the cause afterwards: `tallyctl restart ingest --drain`. It parks the
+stuck batch in the dead-letter table, where it can be inspected later.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Renew it with `tallyctl certs renew api`, then restart the API with `tallyctl restart api`. The restart is not
+optional: until then the old certificate is still served. `tallyctl certs list` shows expiry dates.
